WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:01.940
- [Narrator] The Federal
Reserve has signaled

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:03.500
that it would begin steadily raising

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:05.370
interest rates in mid-March.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.620
- Against a backdrop of elevated inflation

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.230
and a strong labor market,

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.750
our policy has been adapting

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.960
to the evolving economic environment.

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.860
- [Narrator] It's the latest
step the Fed is taking

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:17.820
toward removing stimulus
to bring down inflation

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.800
and marks the first time it
has raised borrowing costs

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.350
since December of 2018.

00:00:23.350 --> 00:00:25.490
Expectations of a faster pace

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.550
of rate rises prompted investors

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:31.450
to change strategies they had
used for nearly two years,

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.840
leading to the worst stock market selloff

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:36.520
since the early days of the pandemic.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:39.740
WSJ personal finance
reporter J.J. McCorvey

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.730
breaks down how the rate
hike could affect mortgages,

00:00:42.730 --> 00:00:45.853
loans, credit cards and
your retirement planning.

00:00:47.546 --> 00:00:50.213
(pensive music)

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.230
- The market's rates have
already been increasing

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:57.320
in anticipation of the Fed rate hikes

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.360
but now they're expected to
go up even more through 2022.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:05.150
However, if you have a
30-year fixed rate mortgage,

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:06.750
you have less reason to worry

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.460
because your rate is locked

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.430
and you likely won't be affected.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.570
- [Narrator] While fixed rate mortgages

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.210
have a set interest rate
for the life of the loan,

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:16.700
repayments on adjustable-rate mortgages

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.840
become more expensive
when interest rates go up.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.070
The rate increase could
also pose challenges

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:24.230
for those shopping for a home.

00:01:24.230 --> 00:01:25.410
- For people who are new home buyers,

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.150
the higher rates could make it harder

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.120
to qualify for a mortgage.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:31.700
- [Narrator] Even small
rate increases can be costly

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.550
if applied to big purchases

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:36.393
when stretched over the
life of a multi-year loan.

00:01:37.289 --> 00:01:40.810
(dramatic music)

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:42.820
- Rising rates will impact both private

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:44.630
and federal student loans.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.530
New interest rates apply to only new loans

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.993
but people who have existing private loans

00:01:48.993 --> 00:01:51.133
might have the option to refinance.

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:53.640
Refinancing student loans

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.310
could be a good option for those

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:57.150
who qualify for a better rate

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.890
based on changes in credit or income.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:01.450
When it comes to auto loans,

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:03.380
similar to fixed rate mortgages,

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:06.150
most won't be affected
if you already have one

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.800
because they usually come
with a fixed interest rate.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.530
- But if you're shopping for a car,

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.270
a new loan with higher interest

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.508
means you'll be paying
a little more money.

00:02:14.508 --> 00:02:17.258
(dramatic music)

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:19.520
Interest rates on credit cards

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.130
are typically not fixed.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.290
In fact, credit card interest rates

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:26.850
change almost immediately
after a Fed rate increase.

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:28.250
If your interest rate rises,

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.218
so could your minimum payment

00:02:30.218 --> 00:02:32.310
and that could make it
harder to pay off debt.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:34.370
- While higher interest
rates means more money

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:35.950
can be earned from savings,

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.520
if you carry a credit card
balance month to month,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.610
a higher interest charge could be applied

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.050
to those balances.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:43.520
Paying balances in full

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.943
will help avoid additional costs.

00:02:45.943 --> 00:02:48.693
(dramatic music)

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:52.160
- If you have an interest
bearing savings account,

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.390
that could mean a little
more money for you

00:02:54.390 --> 00:02:57.370
but how much that changes
depends on your bank.

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:58.830
- [Narrator] The national
average interest rate

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.200
for savings accounts is .06%

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.420
but some banks have savings rates higher

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:05.950
than the national average,

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.080
which will earn savers more money.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.910
- Experts say fixed rate savings,

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:14.240
such as annuities and short-term
bond yields will also rise.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:15.610
This could benefit folks

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.070
who live off interest.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.950
Markets have already
experienced some volatility due

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.030
to the expected rate hike,

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:25.350
which has pushed some
investors towards safer bets.

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:27.780
- [Narrator] Those safer bets
could include dividend stocks

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.110
and gold exchange traded funds,

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:33.243
some of which have outperformed this year.

00:03:33.243 --> 00:03:35.820
(pensive music)

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.870
Federal Reserve Chairman Jerome Powell

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.910
has left the door open to raising rates

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:42.000
at consecutive policy meetings,

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.140
which are held about every six weeks.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:48.060
The Fed hasn't raised rates
at that frequency since 2006.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:50.490
While Powell says he
expects inflation rates

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.980
to decline through the course of the year,

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.550
he admits the economic outlook
remains highly uncertain.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.000
- With this in mind,

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:00.240
we will remain attentive to risks,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.070
including the risk that high inflation

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:04.051
is more persistent than expected

00:04:04.051 --> 00:04:06.620
and are prepared to respond as appropriate

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:08.053
to achieve our goals.

00:04:09.133 --> 00:04:11.800
(pensive music)

